B.Jb=new A.cn("photos","Photo Library","Edge-to-edge photo grid under a glass title bar and a split bottom bar","A photo library, rebuilt with the components. Nothing on it is a bar: the grid starts at pixel row zero and scrolls under the title, so every glass surface always has photographs moving behind it rather than a solid header.\n\nOne material at four sizes \u2014 the same white frost, rim and refraction on a filter circle, a Select pill, a two-tab capsule and a detached search circle. Four surfaces that read as one sheet of glass cut four ways, which is the whole trick of a glass UI: the material is the constant, the shapes are not.\n\nThe page paints from the theme, so its backdrop is whatever `scaffoldBackgroundColor` the app hands it \u2014 light here, and dark if you run it under a dark theme.","// Nothing is a bar: the grid runs edge to edge and the glass\n// floats over it, so there is always a photo behind the frost.\nLiquidGlassView(\n  backgroundWidget: myPhotoGrid,\n  child: Stack(\n    children: [\n      Positioned(top: 0, child: myTitleBar),   // filter + Select\n      Positioned(bottom: 0, child: myTabs),    // capsule + search\n    ],\n  ),\n)","example/lib/showcases/photos_library_page.dart",B.p5,A.b2c(),!0)
B.Jp=new A.cn("lens","Lens","One draggable LiquidGlassLens over a photo, and the style that shapes it","The component everything else is made of. One lens over a photo, inside a LiquidGlassView so the same page runs on the web capture path and on Impeller. Drag it anywhere.\n\nThe panel is a tour of LiquidGlassStyle. The shape: corner style \u2014 circular, squircle, or the continuous curve that collapses to a clean capsule. The blur. The refraction: how hard the edge band bends the photo, and how wide that band is.\n\nRefraction index and refraction width are the two numbers that decide how the glass reads. A narrow strong band is a thick rim; a wide gentle one is a lens. Blur is the one knob that costs a second backdrop pass.","LiquidGlassView(\n  backgroundWidget: const DemoPhoto('socotra_tree_3.jpg'),\n  child: Positioned(\n    left: pos.dx,\n    top: pos.dy,\n    width: 280,\n    height: 176,\n    child: GestureDetector(\n      onPanUpdate: (d) => setState(() => pos += d.delta),\n      child: LiquidGlassLens(\n        style: LiquidGlassStyle(\n          shape: LiquidGlassShape(\n            cornerStyle: LiquidGlassCornerStyle.squircle,\n            cornerRadius: 44,\n          ),\n          blur: 4,\n          refractionIndex: 1.022,\n          refractionWidth: 40,\n        ),\n        child: const Center(child: Text('LiquidGlassLens')),\n      ),\n    ),\n  ),\n)","example/lib/lens_page.dart",B.ak,A.b27(),!0)
B.Js=new A.cn("lens-plain","Liquid glass","One draggable LiquidGlassLens over a photo","The whole package in one widget: a lens inside a LiquidGlassView, refracting the photo behind it, magnifying it and bending it hardest at the edge band. Drag it anywhere.\n\nIt is the lens demo without its style panel \u2014 open that one to take the same glass apart knob by knob.","LiquidGlassView(\n  backgroundWidget: const DemoPhoto(_wallpaperUrl),\n  child: LiquidGlassLens(\n    style: const LiquidGlassStyle(\n      shape: LiquidGlassShape.continuousRoundedRectangle(\n        cornerRadius: 44,\n      ),\n      specular: LiquidGlassLight.specular(thickness: 1.4),\n      glow: LiquidGlassLight.glow(thickness: 1.4),\n      refractionIndex: 1.022,\n      refractionWidth: 40,\n    ),\n    child: const Center(child: Text('LiquidGlassLens')),\n  ),\n)","example/lib/lens_page.dart",B.ak,A.b28(),!1)
B.J5=new A.cn("capture","Capture","The Skia snapshot, and every knob on it","Impeller reads the live backdrop per lens. Skia and the web cannot, so LiquidGlassView snapshots what sits behind the glass and the lenses refract that snapshot. Every control here is a knob on that snapshot.\n\nThe background moves and counts, which is the point. Switch the capture to `frozen` and the number inside the glass stops while the one behind it keeps going: the gap between them is how stale the snapshot has become. `capture once` takes a fresh one.\n\n`pixel ratio` is the snapshot resolution \u2014 the cheapest real win on a slow device. `useSync` picks toImageSync over the async path, and the rate caps how often a new snapshot is taken at all. Impeller takes none of them, and it does not exist on the web.","LiquidGlassView(\n  controller: controller,          // controller.captureOnce()\n  backgroundWidget: myMovingBackground,\n  realTimeCapture: false,          // freeze it\n  pixelRatio: 1.0,                 // snapshot resolution\n  useSync: true,                   // toImageSync\n  refreshRate: LiquidGlassRefreshRate.deviceRefreshRate,\n  child: const LiquidGlassLens(\n    style: LiquidGlassStyle(\n      shape: LiquidGlassShape.continuousRoundedRectangle(\n        cornerRadius: 38,\n      ),\n    ),\n  ),\n)","example/lib/capture_page.dart",B.ak,A.b23(),!0)
B.Jj=new A.cn("shadow","Shadow","The contact shadow that sets glass into the page, on two draggable lenses over a light notes list","A light page, because that is where a shadow shows. Two lenses float over a list of white rows \u2014 a wide card and a round button \u2014 and both wear a LiquidGlassShadow through the style's `shadow`. Drag them across the rows and the dark band under the rim follows.\n\nThe first chip is the argument. Switch the shadow off and the glass floats flat on the page; switch it back on and it sits IN the page. The shadow is cast by a ring straddling the rim, pushed down and blurred, and composited with multiply \u2014 it darkens the white rows under it instead of laying grey over them.\n\nBlur is the softness and the thickness of the casting ring. Opacity is how dark it lands. Offset pushes the ring further down, so the drop beneath grows while the rim contact stays. Inset tucks the shadow inside the glass, for a tighter contact on a small control.","LiquidGlassLens(\n  style: LiquidGlassStyle(\n    shape: const LiquidGlassShape.continuousRoundedRectangle(\n      cornerRadius: 34,\n    ),\n    color: const Color(0x40FFFFFF),\n    shadow: LiquidGlassShadow(\n        blur: blur,          \n        opacity: opacity,    \n        offset: Offset(0, offset),\n        inset: inset,\n        visible: on,\n      ),\n  ),\n  child: const Text('LiquidGlassShadow'),\n)","example/lib/shadow_page.dart",B.ak,A.b2f(),!0)
B.Jk=new A.cn("lite","Lite glass","The material without the shader, on two draggable surfaces over a painted backdrop","No view, no shader, no capture. The card is a LiquidGlassLite; the round button is an ordinary LiquidGlassLens whose style sets `liteGlass: true`, and it draws the same thing \u2014 two routes to one material. Drag either across the backdrop: the frost and the tint follow, and the rim keeps its light.\n\nThe rim is the lens's own specular, the same band from the same numbers, painted over the surface and blended into what is under it, so it reads nothing. Specular thickness is how deep it reaches in from the outline, and the tint chips colour both surfaces.\n\nBlur is the frost, and it is the one backdrop read the glass itself costs; magnification rides in the same filter, a flat zoom with no edge bend. Set blur to zero and magnification to one, and what is left is a few drawVertices calls.","// The widget itself\u2026\nLiquidGlassLite(\n  shape: const LiquidGlassShape.continuousRoundedRectangle(\n    cornerRadius: 34,\n  ),\n  specular: const LiquidGlassLight.specular(thickness: 5),\n  blur: 3,\n  magnification: 1.0,\n  color: const Color(0x33FFFFFF), // the tint, under the rim\n  child: const Text('LiquidGlassLite'),\n)\n\n// \u2026or any lens, told to draw lite in its style.\nLiquidGlassLens(\n  style: const LiquidGlassStyle(liteGlass: true),\n  child: const Icon(Icons.add_rounded),\n)","example/lib/lite_page.dart",B.ak,A.b29(),!0)
B.Jr=new A.cn("blender","Blender","Two draggable tiles fused by one LiquidGlassBlender","Two glass tiles \u2014 each its own lens with its own icon and label \u2014 drawn as one surface by the LiquidGlassBlender around them. Drag them together and they flow into each other through a metaball bridge; pull them apart and they separate again.\n\nThe bridge is not drawn. It falls out of the distance field the moment two members come within about half the smoothness radius of each other, and the slider is that radius. Small, and the tiles stay two hard outlines on one sheet; large, and the pair melts into one shape.\n\nOne shader pass for both, whatever the slider says.","LiquidGlassBlender(\n  smoothness: smoothness,   // the bridge radius\n  style: glass,\n  child: Stack(\n    clipBehavior: Clip.none,\n    children: [\n      for (int i = 0; i < 2; i++)\n        Positioned(\n          left: pos[i].dx,\n          top: pos[i].dy,\n          width: 118,\n          height: 96,\n          child: GestureDetector(\n            onPanUpdate: (d) => setState(() => pos[i] += d.delta),\n            child: LiquidGlassLens(style: glass, child: tile(i)),\n          ),\n        ),\n    ],\n  ),\n)","example/lib/blender_page.dart",B.ak,A.b21(),!0)
B.Jo=new A.cn("button","Button","Two LiquidGlassButtons that flex under the finger","A round button and a wide one over a photo. Both carry a `touch` with the default LiquidGlassFlex, so a press sinks them slightly and a drag stretches the glass toward the finger before it springs back \u2014 the same soft body any lens can wear.\n\nThere is no variant enum. The round one is `icon` alone with width equal to height and corners at half of it; the wide one is `icon` and `label` on a long capsule. `style` is the same LiquidGlassStyle every other component takes \u2014 clear glass here, no blur, a wide refraction band.\n\nPress and hold, then pull sideways.","LiquidGlassButton(\n  icon: Icons.favorite_rounded,\n  width: 88,\n  height: 88,\n  padding: EdgeInsets.zero,\n  style: round,\n  touch: const LiquidGlassTouch.flexing(LiquidGlassFlex()),\n  onPressed: like,\n)\n\nLiquidGlassButton(\n  icon: Icons.add_rounded,\n  label: 'Add to library',\n  width: 340,\n  height: 64,\n  style: wide,\n  touch: const LiquidGlassTouch.flexing(LiquidGlassFlex()),\n  onPressed: add,\n)","example/lib/button_page.dart",B.ak,A.b22(),!0)
B.Ji=new A.cn("appbar","App Bar","A glass capsule the page scrolls under","LiquidGlassAppBar is a floating capsule with the three slots a bar needs \u2014 leading, title, actions \u2014 and nothing else. It lives in a LiquidGlassScaffold, which provides the view it samples, so the grid of tiles here passes straight through it as you scroll.\n\nThe chips flip `centerTitle` and add or remove actions live. The bar re-lays out around whatever it is given; the glass does not care how many icons sit on it.\n\n`width` and `height` are the capsule, and the scaffold takes care of the safe area above it. Give it a `style` to change the material the way you would on any lens.","LiquidGlassScaffold(\n  appBar: LiquidGlassAppBar(\n    width: barWidth,\n    height: 54,\n    centerTitle: true,\n    title: const Text('Library'),\n    leading: IconButton(\n      icon: const Icon(Icons.arrow_back_rounded),\n      onPressed: () => Navigator.of(context).maybePop(),\n    ),\n    actions: [\n      IconButton(icon: const Icon(Icons.search_rounded), onPressed: search),\n      IconButton(icon: const Icon(Icons.tune_rounded), onPressed: filter),\n    ],\n  ),\n  body: GridView.builder(...),\n)","example/lib/app_bar_page.dart",B.ak,A.b1Z(),!0)
B.J7=new A.cn("scaffold","Scaffold","Every scaffold slot, one at a time, over the same captured page","The scaffold IS the glass pipeline. It owns the LiquidGlassView, and everything it places refracts its body, on both engines, with no view to wire up yourself.\n\nWhat it can place is a fixed set of slots, and the chips at the top of the feed put exactly ONE of them on the page: `appBar`, `bottomNavigationBar`, the `bottomNavigationBarAction` beside it, a `floatingActionButton`, the free-floating `lenses` list, and `dialog` \u2014 plus body-only, which is the page with no chrome at all. Same scaffold, same feed, one surface. Nothing in the frame is arguing with the thing you are looking at.\n\nThe picker rides IN the feed rather than over it, so it is part of the body the scaffold captures: whichever slot is showing refracts its own chips. The dialog is the one that answers back \u2014 hand the slot a widget and the scaffold animates it in over a barrier, then tells you through `onDialogDismissed` when to clear it, which is what drops the page back to body-only.\n\nThe feed is a light page with a red glow, so the frosted glass has colour to bend as it scrolls. This is the page to start from for a whole screen.","// One slot at a time: the rest are null.\nLiquidGlassScaffold(\n  body: feed,\n  appBar: slot == Slot.appBar ? DemoHeader(title: 'For you') : null,\n  bottomNavigationBar: slot == Slot.navBar\n      ? LiquidGlassTabBar(\n          items: items,\n          selectedIndex: tab,\n          onChanged: (i) => setState(() => tab = i),\n          style: frost(30),\n        )\n      : null,\n  bottomNavigationBarAction: slot == Slot.action\n      ? LiquidGlassTabBarAction(icon: Icons.add_rounded, size: 60)\n      : null,\n  floatingActionButton: slot == Slot.fab\n      ? LiquidGlassFab.extended(label: const Text('Play all'))\n      : null,\n  // The escape hatch: glass composited over the body, positioned by you.\n  lenses: slot == Slot.lenses ? myLenses : const [],\n  dialog: slot == Slot.dialog ? myDialog : null,\n  onDialogDismissed: () => setState(() => slot = Slot.none),\n)","example/lib/scaffold_page.dart",B.ak,A.b2d(),!0)
B.Jl=new A.cn("alert-dialog","Alert Dialog","Three glass dialogs through showLiquidGlassDialog, in a LiquidGlassScaffold","`showLiquidGlassDialog` is Flutter's dialog route with a glass barrier: it takes a builder, returns a Future, and pops with a value like any showDialog.\n\nThe page is a LiquidGlassScaffold, and that is the part that matters: a dialog opened from a context inside one joins the scaffold's capture, so the panel refracts the photo behind it on every backend. Open it from a bare view and it falls back to a frosted fill.\n\nThree panels: a plain confirm, a destructive one, and a long one with `scrollable: true`. The actions are the stock button glass with a blue tint and no blur \u2014 `copyWith(color:, blur: 0)` on `LiquidGlassButton.defaultStyle` \u2014 and Delete is the same glass in red. The chip toggles `barrierDismissible`.","// Inside a LiquidGlassScaffold, from a Builder's context:\nfinal answer = await showLiquidGlassDialog<String>(\n  context: context,\n  barrierDismissible: false,\n  builder: (context) => LiquidGlassAlertDialog(\n    icon: const Icon(Icons.delete_outline_rounded),\n    title: const Text('Delete 12 photos?'),\n    content: const Text('They go to Recently Deleted for 30 days.'),\n    actions: [\n      LiquidGlassButton(\n        label: 'Cancel',\n        // Blue tint, no blur.\n        style: LiquidGlassButton.defaultStyle.copyWith(\n          color: Color(0x660A84FF),\n          blur: 0,\n        ),\n        onPressed: () => Navigator.of(context).pop('cancel'),\n      ),\n      LiquidGlassButton(\n        label: 'Delete',\n        style: LiquidGlassButton.defaultStyle.copyWith(\n          color: Color(0x80EF4444),\n          blur: 0,\n        ),\n        onPressed: () => Navigator.of(context).pop('deleted'),\n      ),\n    ],\n  ),\n);","example/lib/dialog_page.dart",B.ak,A.b24(),!0)
B.J9=new A.cn("draggable","Draggable","Drag a lens without rebuilding it","LiquidGlassDraggable wraps a widget so a pan moves it. The offset lives in the wrapper, and only a translate rebuilds per frame: the lens inside stays one stable subtree, runs its pipeline once, and keeps sampling the backdrop correctly at wherever it ends up because the renderer resolves its rect through the layer transform.\n\nOne clear disc over a photograph, and nothing else under the glass: no readout painted into the backdrop, no magnification, so what the disc shows is the photograph bent at its rim and lined up across it. `onChanged` reports each offset, read out in the panel; the chip flips `enabled`.\n\nIt inherits whatever the lens it wraps needs, so on the web it sits inside a LiquidGlassView like everything else.","LiquidGlassDraggable(\n  enabled: enabled,\n  onChanged: (offset) => setState(() => disc = offset),\n  child: const SizedBox(\n    width: 168,\n    height: 168,\n    child: LiquidGlassLens(\n      style: clear,\n      child: Center(child: Icon(Icons.open_with_rounded)),\n    ),\n  ),\n)","example/lib/draggable_page.dart",B.ak,A.b25(),!0)
B.Je=new A.cn("scroll-edge","Scroll Edge","A tinted, blurred band over each end of a photo journal","LiquidGlassScrollEdge is the band pinned over the end of a scroll: a tint that fades in from the edge, and one blur pass under it. One backdrop read, one BackdropFilter, at any sigma, on every backend. It never blocks touches.\n\nThe page is a travel journal \u2014 edge-to-edge photos with entries between them \u2014 in a LiquidGlassScaffold, with a glass header on top and a band at each end in the `lenses` slot. Photos dim as they slide under the title, so it stays readable.\n\nSoft \u2014 the default \u2014 feathers the blur across the band so it meets the sharp photos without a seam. Hard leaves it uniform and ends on a line. Neither costs more than the other. Set `blur` to zero and no filter exists in the tree at all.","LiquidGlassScaffold(\n  appBar: const DemoHeader(title: 'Scroll Edge'),\n  body: const _Journal(),\n  lenses: [\n    Positioned(\n      left: 0, right: 0, top: 0,\n      height: padding.top + 130,\n      child: LiquidGlassScrollEdge(\n        edge: LiquidGlassEdge.top,\n        style: LiquidGlassScrollEdgeStyle.soft,\n        blur: 6,\n        color: const Color(0xA6000000),\n      ),\n    ),\n  ],\n)","example/lib/scroll_edge_page.dart",B.ak,A.b2e(),!0)
B.Jf=new A.cn("motion-pill","Motion Pill","The slider's capsule thumb on its own, riding a rail","LiquidGlassMotionPill is the glass thumb the slider carries, without the control around it. Press it and it lifts from its rest size to its active size under your finger; drag along the rail and it squashes from acceleration rather than speed, so a steady pull reads calm and a flick reads thrown; let go and it contracts back where you left it.\n\nThe page feeds it two things per frame \u2014 a `center` whose x follows the finger and whose y is fixed by the rail, and an `active` flag \u2014 and that is the whole contract. The morph, the squash and the rendering are the pill's own. The white capsule is its `cover`, what it looks like at rest, crossfading out as the glass arrives.\n\nThis is the piece to build a custom control on when you want the same thumb as the shipped ones and your own track.","Listener(\n  onPointerDown: (e) { moveTo(e.localPosition.dx); setState(() => active = true); },\n  onPointerMove: (e) => moveTo(e.localPosition.dx),\n  onPointerUp: (_) => setState(() => active = false),\n  child: LiquidGlassMotionPill(\n    center: Offset(x, railY),          // only x moves\n    active: active,\n    restSize: const Size(84, 46),\n    activeSize: const Size(109, 53),\n    style: glass,\n    cover: const DecoratedBox(\n      decoration: BoxDecoration(\n        color: Colors.white,\n        borderRadius: BorderRadius.all(Radius.circular(999)),\n      ),\n    ),\n  ),\n)","example/lib/motion_pill_page.dart",B.ak,A.b2b(),!0)
B.Jh=new A.cn("blend","Blending Liquid Glasses","Drag glass shapes together to fuse, over a photo","A photo fills the screen and two draggable glass shapes float on top, fused by a LiquidGlassBlender: drag them together and they merge into one liquid surface, then snap apart as you separate them. Each member keeps its own corner style through the merge \u2014 the circle stays a circle, the squircle stays a squircle.\n\nNo blur anywhere on the group style. This is clear refraction over the photograph, which is what makes the fusion legible: a smooth gradient bends into another smooth gradient invisibly, but the detail in a photo visibly bows.\n\nThe page is wrapped in a LiquidGlassView, which is what lets it run in your browser at all: on Skia the merged surface refracts the view's captured background, on Impeller it reads the live backdrop and takes no capture. Outside a view, a blend is Impeller-only.","LiquidGlassView(\n  backgroundWidget: myPhoto,\n  child: LiquidGlassBlender(\n    smoothness: 58,\n    style: _groupStyle,          // clear glass, optical rim\n    child: Stack(\n      clipBehavior: Clip.none,\n      children: [\n        // Each member keeps its OWN shape through the merge.\n        myCircleLens,            // roundedRectangle, r = h / 2\n        mySquircleLens,          // squircle\n      ],\n    ),\n  ),\n)","example/lib/lens_image_page.dart",B.ak,A.b20(),!0)
B.Jc=new A.cn("morph","Morph Component","LiquidGlassMorph \u2014 glass that fits whatever you put in it, with the anchor switchable live","LiquidGlassMorph is glass that measures whatever you put in it and flows to the new size. Tap the surface and its child swaps from a search button to a results panel; the glass is never told a size, so adding a row to the panel grows the glass with nothing to keep in sync.\n\nMid-travel it is not a resize. The surface is two blender blobs drawn as one sheet \u2014 the shape it is leaving and the shape it is becoming \u2014 so on the way it has a waist, and the corner curve swaps mid-flight. `alignment` is the corner it grows out of: bottom-right opens the way a real toolbar control has to, and the chips below switch it live without touching the code.\n\nThe motion presets \u2014 fluid, anchored pop, droplet, calm, plain \u2014 are the whole choice a caller makes. Plain is the one-lens kind: no second blob, no blender, a single spring resizing one outline, so the smoothness slider has nothing to fillet. Anchor and motion each get their own scrolling row, and the two sliders left under them drive the material: blur, and the smoothness of the neck between the two blobs. Fold the panel away with the arrow in its header \u2014 the two bottom anchors open into the space it was taking.","GestureDetector(\n  onTap: swap,\n  child: LiquidGlassMorph(\n    // The corner the surface grows out of. Change it and the\n    // SAME code opens the other way.\n    alignment: Alignment.bottomRight,\n    style: _glass,\n    motion: LiquidGlassMorphMotion.fluid,\n    // No width. No height. The glass measures whatever is in\n    // it and flows to fit \u2014 swapping the child IS the morph.\n    child: expanded ? const _Panel() : const _Button(),\n  ),\n)","example/lib/morph_page.dart",B.ak,A.b2a(),!0)
B.J6=new A.cn("tabbar","Tab Bar","One wide frosted capsule on a light page, with all three pill modes a chip apart","LiquidGlassTabBar is the whole bottom bar \u2014 the glass capsule, the items, and whatever marks the selected one. There is no second bar widget, which is why the chips under the header can swap the selection tier live without rebuilding anything else: they are all `pillStyle.mode` on this one bar.\n\nBOTH is the glass-refracting pill on every renderer \u2014 the selection becomes a SECOND refracting surface that morphs between tabs and bends the bar itself. Watch the hairlines behind it. IMPELLERONLY is that same pill where it is free and a flat sliding highlight everywhere else, from one line. NONE is the flat capsule everywhere: one lens, one backdrop read for the whole bar.\n\nWARNING \u2014 do not ship `both` on Skia. On Impeller the glass pill is very fast, excellent performance, use it freely. On Skia it is an experiment: you are on Skia right now, because the web never gets Impeller, so `both` here is a second full-page capture on top of the bar's own \u2014 heavy, and this is the place to watch it cost something. `impellerOnly` renders exactly like `none` here, which is the point of the mode: ship that one.\n\nThe pill needs a pipeline to run in, which is why the page is a LiquidGlassScaffold: the scaffold captures its own body and hands it to the bar. Every tab is drawn by `iconBuilder` rather than an IconData, so a selected glyph can bloom \u2014 the builder is handed the colour the bar already resolved for the layer it is drawing, which is what makes the red wipe on as the pill arrives instead of switching under it.","LiquidGlassScaffold(\n  body: _OnAirFeed(title: title),\n  bottomNavigationBar: LiquidGlassTabBar(\n    items: _items,\n    selectedIndex: _index,\n    onChanged: (i) => setState(() => _index = i),\n    width: barWidth,\n    pillStyle: LiquidGlassTabPillStyle(\n      // The only line the chips change:\n      //   both         -> glass pill on every renderer\n      //                   (experimental + heavy on Skia; don't ship it)\n      //   impellerOnly -> glass pill only where it is free (ship this)\n      //   none         -> the flat sliding capsule everywhere\n      mode: _mode,\n      rest: _restStyle,\n    ),\n  ),\n)","example/lib/tab_bar_page.dart",B.ak,A.b2j(),!0)
B.Jq=new A.cn("touch","Touch","Press and drag a glass list \u2014 it deforms without moving","Pass a `touch:` and the lens becomes a soft body. Press it and it swells under your finger; drag it and it elongates along the pull, pinches in the cross axis, leans after your thumb, then springs back with a wobble.\n\nThe card never moves. Its footprint in layout is unchanged, so nothing around it shifts. The four edges spring independently, so the half nearest your finger deforms more than the far half \u2014 the asymmetry a scale transform cannot produce.\n\nThe rows inside are ordinary widgets. They are the lens child, so the deformation carries them instead of each one deforming on its own \u2014 that is childFollow, and it is why the text never re-wraps mid-gesture.","LiquidGlassLens(\n  touch: const LiquidGlassTouch(\n    // .subtle() \xb7 .pronounced() \xb7 .uniform()\n    flex: LiquidGlassFlex(),\n  ),\n  style: const LiquidGlassStyle(\n    shape: LiquidGlassShape.continuousRoundedRectangle(\n      cornerRadius: 30,\n    ),\n  ),\n  child: myList,\n)","example/lib/touch_page.dart",B.ak,A.b2k(),!0)
B.Jm=new A.cn("toggle","Switch","Three glass switches whose thumb rides your finger","LiquidGlassSwitch, and the thing that makes it not a repaint of the platform switch: the thumb is carried, not snapped.\n\nTap one and it toggles at once, the thumb gliding across. Press and DRAG the thumb and it rides your finger 1:1, rubber-banding by the square root of the overrun past either rest position. Drag toward the far side and stop short of it \u2014 it flips early, under your held finger, and the track colour cross-fades there. Drag back and it flips again.\n\nPress, hold a moment without moving, release: it still toggles. A drag that never moved is a tap, and a control that forgets that is a control people fight.","LiquidGlassSwitch(\n  value: on,\n  onChanged: (v) => setState(() => on = v),\n)","example/lib/switch_page.dart",B.ak,A.b2i(),!0)
B.Jg=new A.cn("slider","Slider","Blue glass sliders \u2014 the same thumb, carried along a track","The same glass thumb the switch carries between two rest positions, carried along a continuous track instead.\n\nDrag a thumb and it stretches as it launches and squashes as it brakes into a stop. The deformation is driven by ACCELERATION, not speed: at constant velocity it sits undeformed, which is why a slow careful drag reads as calm and a flick reads as thrown.\n\nTap anywhere on a track and the thumb travels there and lands with the same squash a drag-release gives it. Press and hold without moving and it lifts into glass and stays there until you let go.","LiquidGlassSlider(\n  value: value,\n  onChanged: (v) => setState(() => value = v),\n)","example/lib/slider_page.dart",B.ak,A.b2h(),!0)
B.J4=new A.cn("fab","FAB & Alert Dialog","Liquid glass FABs and animated glass alert dialogs","LiquidGlassFab and LiquidGlassAlertDialog, both wired to something you can press.\n\nThe FAB switches between its plain and extended forms live, so you can watch the capsule grow around the label rather than swap for a different widget. Visibility is a first-class parameter and not an Opacity: hiding glass this way stops the pass entirely, where an Opacity keeps paying for a surface nobody can see.\n\nThe dialog route is `showLiquidGlassDialog` \u2014 the ordinary Flutter dialog route with a glass barrier. The actions inside it are LiquidGlassButtons, and the confirm button shows how to tint one by overriding `color` on the shared default style rather than authoring a whole new one.","showLiquidGlassDialog(\n  context: context,\n  builder: (context) => LiquidGlassAlertDialog(\n    icon: const Icon(Icons.auto_awesome_rounded),\n    title: const Text('Liquid Glass Dialog'),\n    content: const Text('Refracting over the page behind it.'),\n    actions: [\n      LiquidGlassButton(label: 'Cancel', onPressed: pop),\n      LiquidGlassButton(\n        label: 'Confirm',\n        // Tint the shared default, do not author a new style.\n        style: LiquidGlassButton.defaultStyle.copyWith(\n          color: Color(0x607C5CFF),\n        ),\n        onPressed: confirm,\n      ),\n    ],\n  ),\n)","example/lib/fab_dialog_demo.dart",B.ak,A.b26(),!0)
B.Ja=new A.cn("sheet","Sheets","Glass sheets that hug, detent or sit on the bottom edge","`showLiquidGlassSheet` is Flutter's own `showModalBottomSheet` with the glass where its filled Material used to be \u2014 every parameter of theirs forwarded, plus the style. Three buttons on this page present three shapes of the same surface.\n\nThe content sheet names no detents, so it is exactly as tall as the column inside it \u2014 the shape of an action sheet. The expandable one is a DraggableScrollableSheet with a bare LiquidGlassSheet inside its builder: the glass sits within what resizes, so it grows and shrinks with the drag and snaps between half the screen and nearly all of it. The attached one is full width on the bottom edge, only its top corners in frame.\n\nThe panel itself is a plain widget, which is the part to remember: the route is one way to present it, not the only one. Anything that owns the motion \u2014 a persistent bottom area, a side panel \u2014 can hold a LiquidGlassSheet directly.","final choice = await showLiquidGlassSheet<String>(\n  context: context,\n  header: const _SheetTitle('Share'),\n  // No detents named: the sheet is exactly as tall as this\n  // column. For the bottom-edge form, pass\n  //   anchor: LiquidGlassSheetAnchor.attached,\n  builder: (context) => Column(\n    mainAxisSize: MainAxisSize.min,\n    children: [\n      for (final action in actions) _actionTile(action),\n      LiquidGlassButton(\n        label: 'Cancel',\n        width: double.infinity,\n        onPressed: () => Navigator.of(context).pop(),\n      ),\n    ],\n  ),\n)","example/lib/sheet_field_page.dart",B.ak,A.b2g(),!0)
B.J8=new A.cn("batch","Batch","A wall of glass cards on one shared backdrop read","A scrolling wall of glass cards, every one a LiquidGlassLens, all inside one LiquidGlassBatch. On Impeller the read of the backdrop is the expensive part and it is per lens \u2014 twenty cards mean twenty readbacks a frame. The batch tags them all with one shared key, so the engine copies once and every member samples it.\n\nMembers stay separate sheets of glass: each keeps its own shape, its own style, its own child. The batch only makes them share the read, which is why there is no limit on how many there can be \u2014 that is the difference from a group, which fuses its members into one surface and caps out at eight. The first card here is deliberately wrapped in `LiquidGlassBatch.exclude`, so it reads the backdrop on its own while everything under it shares.\n\nYou are on Skia right now, where a LiquidGlassView already captures its background once for every lens inside it \u2014 so the batch is inert here rather than wrong, and the same tree runs on both backends. The raster HUD at the top is where the batch shows its work on Impeller. The one rule either way: members must not overlap, because no member can see another member's glass in the shared copy.","// One wrapper, any number of lenses inside it.\nLiquidGlassBatch(\n  child: ListView.builder(\n    itemCount: cardCount,\n    itemBuilder: (context, i) => LiquidGlassLens(\n      style: cardStyle,\n      child: Card(i),\n    ),\n  ),\n)\n\n// A member that must keep a read of its own opts back out:\nLiquidGlassBatch.exclude(child: myTabBar)","example/lib/batch_page.dart",B.ak,A.b2_(),!0)
B.Jt=new A.cn("adaptivity","Adaptivity","Every glass surface judges the photo behind ITSELF, so they can disagree","Glass reads as glass by agreeing with its backdrop: smoked over a dark photo, milky over a white page, with the icons and text on it inverting to match. Give a style an `adaptivity` and both happen on their own, from the actual pixels behind that surface.\n\nThis is the default, with NO coupling of any kind. The round back button, the bare centred title, the chevron pill and the scroll-edge band all carry the same palettes and each samples the background directly behind ITSELF. So they can disagree \u2014 and that is correct: a title over a dark photo while the pill beside it sits over light paper SHOULD wear different palettes. Scroll slowly and watch each flip on its own.\n\nThe title is bare text with no glass under it at all; it adapts through LiquidGlassAdaptiveContent. Nothing samples pixels unless a view opts in, and ONE deliberately tiny capture \u2014 pixel ratio 0.05, eight per second \u2014 serves every adaptive surface in that view.\n\nThe panel at the bottom is that whole rule, live. VERDICT moves the dark/light split and the hysteresis band the two thresholds open (equal thresholds = no band, just a split), plus the crossfade. SAMPLING is the single capture itself: scale it down until the verdicts start going wrong, or drop it to one sample a second and watch every palette lag the scroll.","LiquidGlassView(\n  backgroundWidget: myFeed,\n  // THE switch. No sampling anywhere on the page without it.\n  adaptiveSampling: const LiquidGlassAdaptiveSampling(),\n  child: LiquidGlassLens(\n    style: const LiquidGlassStyle(\n      adaptivity: LiquidGlassAdaptivity(\n        glassColorOnDark: Color(0x33000000),\n        contentColorOnDark: Colors.white,\n        glassColorOnLight: Color(0x8AFFFFFF),\n        contentColorOnLight: Colors.black,\n      ),\n    ),\n    child: myChrome,\n  ),\n)","example/lib/adaptivity_page.dart",B.ak,A.b1W(),!0)
B.Jd=new A.cn("adaptivity-advanced","Adaptivity \u2014 Advanced","An area samples once and publishes on a link; the rest follow it","Per-surface adaptivity is right until two surfaces sit over different pixels and still have to agree. A header and a footer that disagree read as broken.\n\nTwo pieces fix that, and the button on the page flips between them live. LiquidGlassAdaptiveArea wraps the header and samples ONE region; everything inside it follows that verdict with no wiring at all. LiquidGlassAdaptivityLink is for a follower that cannot sit inside the area \u2014 the area publishes its verdict there, and the button, which lives in another slot entirely, carries the same link and mirrors it on the same frame.\n\nThe nav bar deliberately does NOT take the link. It judges its own capsule in both modes, so there is always an unlinked surface to compare the group against. Scroll until the header sits over a photo and the button over paper \u2014 that is where the two modes stop looking the same.","// One region sampled; everything under it follows.\nLiquidGlassAdaptiveArea(\n  link: myLink,              // ...and published here\n  child: myHeader,\n)\n\n// A follower in another slot, mirroring the same verdict.\nLiquidGlassLens(\n  style: const LiquidGlassStyle(\n    adaptivity: LiquidGlassAdaptivity(link: myLink),\n  ),\n  child: myButton,\n)","example/lib/adaptivity_advanced_page.dart",B.ak,A.b1X(),!0)
B.Jn=new A.cn("adaptivity-controller","Adaptivity \u2014 Controller","Palettes hold while scrolling, adaptOnce() when it settles",'The same photo feed and glass chrome as the plain adaptivity page, but the whole adaptive group hangs off one LiquidGlassAdaptivityController with `enabled: false`. While the feed moves fast every palette stays frozen \u2014 no verdict flips mid-fling.\n\n"Settled" is judged by SPEED, not by touch. Lifting your finger into a fling changes nothing; once the motion decays to a crawl, adaptOnce() takes exactly one look and every surface \u2014 header band, title, chevron pill, nav bar, status icons \u2014 animates to the settled content together.\n\nOne look on entry, one per settled scroll, zero captures in between. This is the page to copy if the sampling itself is what you cannot afford, or if a page that changes its mind mid-animation is what you cannot afford.',"final c = LiquidGlassAdaptivityController(enabled: false);\n\n// Frozen while the feed moves...\nNotificationListener<ScrollNotification>(\n  onNotification: (n) {\n    // ...and judged by SPEED, not by touch: a fling is\n    // still moving after the finger has gone.\n    if (settledFor(n)) c.adaptOnce();\n    return false;\n  },\n  child: myFeed,\n)","example/lib/adaptivity_controller_page.dart",B.ak,A.b1Y(),!0)
